* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files need CRLF line endings to run reliably, and
# scripts/tests/test_launcher_line_endings.sh checks every line break in each
# *.bat blob. Under `-text`, `git add` stores a .bat file's CRLF bytes
# unchanged in the blob. Under `text`, with any `eol` value, it would store
# LF. To show it, run this command in a clone, in a POSIX shell such as Git
# Bash on Windows. It applies to CRLF bytes the conversion that `git add`
# applies to a new file at start_windows.bat, then prints the stored blob:
#   printf 'a\r\nb\r\n' | git hash-object -w --stdin --path=start_windows.bat | xargs git cat-file -p | od -c
# Under `-text` the output shows a `\r` before each `\n`. Under `text`,
# `text eol=crlf`, or `text eol=lf` it shows no `\r`.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text

# Shell/command launchers must stay LF
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
